c school  education in person and this is going  back to the strike of october 2019 it  just seems like a power play  very inappropriate our kids are being  held hostage  and you know adults can have  disagreements but keep the kids out of  it they need their education they need  their services they need all of their  supports extracurricular activities and  we're just going to go further and  further behind  jackie i want to break down alberto's  point just a little bit here are the  demands that the union is making so this  is a showdown showdown over what uh so  they've already been vaccinated already  all of those things have already been  provided now they want negative pcr  tests  for anyone who returns to school they  want 300 testing sites which we know we  don't have enough of uh right now they  want k  special medical grade mask for all staff  and students and then the principals  need to have the authority to take  classes remote at what point jackie are  these demands i mean  what if everybody else who had a job  other than teaching under the shield of  a union could make these types of  demands who deemed themselves essential  and really are essential for kids i mean  why are we not  doing more to call out the unions for  just not working  well i think well first of all they are  working right i mean i've i've had to  pivot one of my colleges they're not  working  they're not working they're not they're  literally not working the kids are not  in the class they're not showing up 75  percent of them voted not to show up  um well i i've taught online and let me  tell you it's a lot more work than than  than teaching in the classroom but it's  no i think  i had kids on a zoom call yesterday in  my class and they're staring like  zombies there's no way they're getting  even if teachers are pedaling harder  than ever there's no way they're getting  the quality of education they get in the  classroom teachers know that you're an  educator you know that's not of course  not remote is not is not a better option  than in person  i think that um but i i also don't think  it's unrealistic to ask teachers what  measures would be appropriate for our  kids to return to school one of the  things we're not talking about here in  chicago is such our low vaccination  rates among the students themselves it's  something like 16 percent  and um you know in that jackie the  students are not affected i mean at what  point do teachers look at the side  now we know that with omicron teachers  are just as likely to get infected as  someone who is unvaccinated at this  point and kids have never been affected  by covin 19. so what's the fear factor  that are that are that that has infected  teachers where they're now saying the  kids aren't vaccinated which the parents  should get to choose so we won't go back  to the classroom like where does it end  does it ever end i mean the pediatric  icus are full of kids with covet and i  think that if you have i have three kids  who all have many of which have been  others  so  did you invite me on here to yell at me  no i expected  i mean  this is an interesting debate because  you're arguing for  some level of online um education that  the unions really aren't uh part of the  problem here and that kids need to get  vaccinated to solve it there's plenty of  kids uh that are sick that's true but  vast majority of them are not affected  by covet here's what i'm arguing for i'm  arguing for the two sides to come  together and come up with a resolution  some kind of resolution so that my kids  aren't caught in the middle of it i mean  i think alberto is right we're we're  seeing this political fight happening in  chicago it happens time and time again  and the kids get caught in the middle  between the rhetoric on both sides and  i'm tired of that i'm exhausted i'm  exhausted trying to parent my kids and  do my job i've been doing this for over  for almost two years now i want my kids  in school i want them to be safe in  school and if they're not going to be in  school i want them to be able to log on  to a computer as ugly as that is and as  hard it as it is i have an autistic kid  my autistic son does not do well with  remote learning he needs to be in the  classroom i want to be him to be in the  classroom when it's safe  alberto last word  i'm a former teacher a former principal  this is inappropriate and unacceptable i  love teachers i'm pro-teacher my  family's pro-teacher but there is a  growing resentment with the teachers  union i think it's time we need to start  having conversations about  accountability  what is the chicago public school  teachers union going to do to make up  for all of this lost learning  how are we going to hold them  accountable they're receiving their  salaries their health benefits other  benefits how are we going to start to  hold them accountable and then finally  we heard from mayor lightfoot but i'd  like to hear from our local state and  national leaders where do you stand with  the parents and families the children of  chicago public schools or with the  teachers union i'd like to know where  president biden stands on this issue  yeah unions are being exposed for what  they really are which are political  machines that protect uh their  priorities and don't necessarily put  kids and parents first at all  jackie and alberto thank you very much  for your time appreciate it  thank you thank you good luck to kids in  chicago it's amazing

